            <description><![CDATA[<style>
:root { color-scheme: light dark; }
body { font-family: -apple-system, 'Helvetica Neue', sans-serif; font-size: 13px; color: #1a1a1a; margin: 12px; }
@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) { body { color: #e6e6e6; } }
h2 { font-size: 15px; margin: 0 0 8px; } ul { margin: 4px 0 10px; padding-left: 20px; } li { margin-bottom: 3px; }
</style>
<h2>Asobi Review 0.8 (build 730)</h2>
<ul>
<li><b>Clip info (&#8984;I).</b> A few quiet lines under the top bar say what the file on screen is: its codec or camera, where it lives, the original&#8217;s size, rate and length, the color space it is being read through and why, its size and data rate (Mbps and MB/s), and &#8212; when an optimized or developed copy is what you are watching &#8212; that too. Also in the eye menu; the lines can be copied.</li>
<li><b>23.976 stays 23.976.</b> Optimized media was written at a rounded frame rate, so a 23.976 shot&#8217;s stand-in played and read as 24 fps. New stand-ins keep the exact rate, and existing ones are corrected as they load &#8212; nothing needs re-making.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
